Recommendations for the development of a "Hedging Policy"

Hedging Policy: Definition
The decision-making process for hedging against market risks must stem from a framework document, in this case, the "Hedging Policy." This document must be generally approved by the Board of Directors or an equivalent management body of the economic operator. The definition of the Hedging Policy is based primarily on:
✓ Identification of risks;
✓ Quantification of risk exposure;
✓ Determination of eligible hedging markets and instruments;
✓ The decision-making process;
✓ Monitoring and reporting.

1.

Identification of Risks
The economic operator must identify all risks to which it is exposed in connection with its activity. Once identified, the risks are categorized and prioritized. Example: An economic operator finances itself in foreign currency at an interest rate indexed to a reference rate to import zinc is exposed to the following risks:
o Variation in the foreign currency/dinar exchange rate;
o Variation in the reference interest rate;
o Fluctuation in zinc prices. Defining the moment of risk exposure also allows for the determination of the true hedging horizon. Consequently, it is important to thoroughly analyze the activity to define the risk-generating operation and its different parameters, namely the production or supply schedule, the amount bought or sold, the volumes, and the periods, etc.

